Fuchsia FIDL, OpenThread, and Matter on 5BSD
==========================================

Status
------
This is a source import and initial native port. It DOES NOT replace the
capability service protocols, install a Matter service, or enable a radio.
Native libfidl library/header/manual build targets and package registration
exist and the optional SDK passes staged generation/link/run tests.
Full world/package and external tool dependency validation remain open.

The imported FIDL compiler, formatter, linter, C++ generator, and Rust
generator build on 5BSD. The native C++ runtime and generated C ABI now
work over libchannel using upstream natural types and codecs. No Zircon
kernel, syscall emulation, dispatcher, or Component Manager is linked.
The 66 native runtime tests, C11 integration test, and 36 generator contract
checks pass, including the ASan/UBSan variant. Eight dependency-graph checks
and a three-library C round trip also pass with the installed/extracted SDK. LeakSanitizer is unavailable
on this host; explicit descriptor-leak checks pass. See lib/libfidl/README
and libfidl_bsd(3) for ownership rules and the supported schema subset.
Rust runtime bindings remain unported. All 2,203 enabled compiler tests pass, including
one local regression; four upstream disabled tests remain disabled.
The Go IR, C++ generator, Rust generator, and end-to-end compiler helper
tests pass. The fd transport experiment passes across fork/cap_enter,
including kernel sender attribution, rights attenuation, and refusal to
transfer a one-hop descriptor again. It uses the upstream transaction
header and wire handle marker; it is not a generated binding or runtime.

OpenThread simulation, CLI, NCP, and radio targets build. All 84 configured CTest tests pass (including the 83 registered
upstream unit-test executables). Physical radio operation is not validated.
The POSIX daemon and ot-ctl build on 5BSD, using base libedit and
OpenThread's own UDP stack. The host-socket UDP backend remains disabled: BSD does not provide Linux's
SO_BINDTODEVICE or Apple's IPV6_BOUND_IF. A correct port must preserve both
receive and transmit interface scoping; renaming a socket option is not
sufficient. Fuchsia lowpan/OpenThread integration source is retained but
its Fuchsia services are not built or run.

Matter's connectedhomeip source, tests, and tooling are retained. The
native Matter device/controller platform, commissioning, BLE, DNS-SD,
persistent storage, and capability-service integration remain unported.
SUBMODULES.5BSD.json records the 79 upstream gitlinks; they are not populated.
All 120 standalone Matter IDL tests pass. The test runner exercises parser,
compatibility,
XML conversion, and code generation, not a commissioned Matter device.

Source selection and licenses
-----------------------------
contrib/fuchsia is a selected export at commit
3c0275544589e9bc156739d210052e8174ae9c60, not an F31 release checkout.
It includes tools/fidl, C++ bindings and wire support, host support,
lowpan/OpenThread integration, selected protocol definitions, compiler
documentation/test data, and notices. Fuchsia is BSD-2-Clause with a
separate PATENTS grant; the FXL ICU UTF helper compiled into fidlc is under
the ICU license; go-cmp v0.7.0 (Go tests only) is BSD-3-Clause.

contrib/openthread is commit 0542780e3ac117196b77a60abc1052e4c3cdad12,
including its tests and exact Mbed TLS and framework submodule pins.
OpenThread is BSD-3-Clause, with file-specific third-party notices.
Mbed TLS and its framework are dual Apache-2.0 OR GPL-2.0-or-later;
this integration selects Apache-2.0. tcplp includes BSD TCP code.

contrib/connectedhomeip is commit d2a28e75da517d7aa5558244fcd2487c7239d02e.
The primary license is Apache-2.0, with LICENSE and NOTICE retained.
Do not label the entire import BSD or uniformly Apache: inipp has its own
MIT license, and embedded FreeRTOS configuration examples contain additional
FreeRTOS/GPL-exception and Silicon Labs notices. In particular, review
examples/platform/silabs/freertos_config/FreeRTOSConfig.h and the bl602,
bl702, and bl702l FreeRTOSConfig.h examples. Those embedded examples are
not built or installed by this integration. Preserve individual notices
and re-review the selected files before adding an installed target.

Native build and tests
----------------------
All object files belong under /usr/obj. These commands do not install,
activate services, publish packages, or reboot. Native compilation uses
all logical CPUs. The host tools require Python 3.12+, C++20, base OpenSSL,
the installed RE2 development files, and the existing contrib/googletest.
RE2 is currently a host-tool dependency; a base bootstrap build still
needs a maintained in-tree dependency policy.

  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-fidl.py --test
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-generators.py --go /path/to/go --test
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/test-fd.py
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-runtime.py --test
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-runtime.py --test --sanitize
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/test-installed.py
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-openthread.py --test
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/build-openthread.py --platform posix

The Thread scripts accept --cmake and --ctest. CMake 4.x requires the
compatibility policy argument already supplied by the script. The optional
unifdefall reduction is disabled because it leaves unresolved feature
conditions after removing the core configuration include on this host.
The unmodified configuration is compiled instead; crypto is not disabled.

Matter tooling dependencies stay in an object-directory virtualenv:

  python3.12 -m venv /usr/obj/5bsd-matter-tools
  /usr/obj/5bsd-matter-tools/bin/python -m pip install \
      -r tools/build/fuchsia/requirements-matter-tools.txt
  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/test-matter.py

Validation logs for the initial port are in
/usr/obj/5bsd-fuchsia-import/; compiler XML is in
/usr/obj/5bsd-fidl/fidlc-test.xml. The initial OpenThread run directly
executed every add_test entry from tests/unit/CTestTestfile.cmake with
120-second timeouts and per-test logs. The maintained runner uses CTest.

Refresh
-------
sources.json locks URLs, exact revisions, archive hashes, and metadata.
Gitiles changes tar timestamps even for an immutable commit. For those
archives the importer verifies a canonical SHA256 over sorted entry names,
types, permissions, contents, and symlink targets instead of timestamps.
The original archive SHA256 remains recorded as provenance. GitHub archives
and individual notice files are verified by their byte SHA256.

  python3.12 tools/build/fuchsia/import.py \
      --dest /usr/obj/5bsd-contrib-refresh --patch

The destination must be empty. Review the staged diff before updating
/usr/src; never re-extract over local changes. patches/ records the BSD
host definition, the lexer SOF-span fix, OpenThread reset support, libedit
include spelling, and the explicit IPv6 header. Update sources.json,
UPSTREAM*.json, patches, and contrib/README.5BSD together, then rerun tests.

IPC backend and remaining migration
-----------------------------------
lib/libfidl combines upstream FIDL natural codecs with libchannel's native
queues, kqueue readiness, cancellation, kernel attribution, and request
lifecycle. tools/build/fuchsia/generate-bsd.py produces C++ adapters and,
with --c, a C ABI implemented behind opaque C types. Only supported natural
types and native adapters are published, not Fuchsia messaging stubs.
The generated C application test compiles as C11.

The backend has explicit descriptor ownership, rights/type checks, late-reply
cleanup, txid/native-token separation, and epitaph handling. Native capability
policy remains the authority. BSDTime/libtimecmp now use FIDL in the source
tree, retaining the public C ABI; production activation and VM validation remain. The whole capability plane still needs VM
validation after service migration, followed by base build/package integration.

Native C/C++ bindings support open, ajar, and closed protocols, including
flexible interactions. Rust runtime bindings remain an implementation gap. Zircon-only
object types require an actual native contract; unsupported mappings fail
closed. Read lib/libfidl/README for exact support and ownership semantics.

The book chapter docs/book/src/develop/fidl.md and fidl(7)/libfidl_bsd(3)
document the current backend. fidl(7) is registered for runtime-man and libfidl_bsd(3) for libfidl-man.
The runtime and optional host SDK have native build/install targets; full
world/release integration remains open. Traditional Unix
IPC remains available.

Native generator changes: patches/fuchsia-native.patch and
patches/fuchsia-native-headers.patch. update-runtime-headers.py maintains the
installed support-header inventory; --check detects stale manifests.

Build-time generation: buildworld builds fidlc from fidlc/Makefile in its
bootstrap-tools stage (patches/fuchsia-fidlc-bootstrap.patch replaces RE2
and OpenSSL with <regex> and libmd) and lib/libfidl/fidl-bindings.mk runs
fidlgen-build.py for every FIDL_BINDINGS set. fidlgen_natural.py ports the
fidlgen_cpp model the native backend uses and runs upstream's
fidlgen_cpp/codegen templates through gotemplate.py, a Go text/template
interpreter, so the Go fidlgen_cpp is not built. Python 3.12 is a build-only
prerequisite (Makefile.inc1 _python_check, PYTHON=). Nothing generated is
checked in; check-bindings generates every set twice and compares.

Binary SDK staging
------------------
The runtime's native Makefile installs shared/static libraries, public C/C++
headers and fidl.pc. The optional tools/build/fuchsia/Makefile installs the
matching fidlc, fidl-format, fidl-lint, fidlgen-bsd and its private Python
generator and templates. Its bootstrap/check targets use the existing build
scripts and PYTHON (GO only for the optional gidl/Rust generators);
installation consumes already built files in FIDL_TOOLS_OBJ (default
/usr/obj/5bsd-fidl). No install step fetches code. Use a DESTDIR under /usr/obj
for staging. The libfidl-tools subpackage is opt-in with FIDL_SDK=yes after
staging these files; normal world packaging does not require host tools.
Host tools require Python 3.12, and fidl-lint RE2, from ports. External dependency/release
packaging validation remains open; this is not a published toolchain package.

The installed test compiles schemas with the staged tools and runs C11/C++
consumers with shared and static runtimes. It checks compiler dependency files
for source-tree leakage and ELF dependencies for the intended link mode.
The C binary uses cc to link; fidl.pc supplies the C++ support library.
Installed support headers are the source include closure of native headers
and supported generator templates (79 upstream headers currently), excluding
unrelated dispatcher APIs. update-runtime-headers.py --check verifies it.

Native patches also preserve BSD's __WEAK header macro, check upstream handle
metadata allocation before use, close transferred descriptors on failure, and
initialize vector<bool> decode temporaries (fuchsia-natural-bool.patch).
Sanitizer and fuzz builds pattern-fill locals so uninitialized reads reach
UBSan deterministically.
Fault-injection tests exercise encoding and decoding allocation failures.

Archive entries use a stable extraction timestamp after content verification;
Gitiles export timestamps can vary or be ahead of the build clock. This keeps
unchanged imports from forcing perpetual recompilation. Run tests/import_test.py
for timestamp, corrupted-cache, and unsafe-path regressions.

Shared-library schema generation uses repeated --dependency-json inputs to
fidlgen-bsd. Dependency order is automatic; types-only libraries export C
headers, and composed methods retain the base protocol's ordinals. Compile
each generated library's natural_types.cc, common_types.cc and c/bsd.cc once.
The library_test.py checks graph rejection, public headers, imported resource
round trips and composed/base interoperability in native, SDK and package runs.

Native tests and CI
-------------------
The standard lib/libfidl/tests Makefile installs C and (with MK_GOOGLETEST)
C++ tests plus a Kyuafile, owned by libfidl-tests. They run without host tools.
Their bindings are generated into the object tree during the build (see
"Build-time generation" above); test-bindings.py (make check-bindings, part
of make check) checks that generation is deterministic.

After bootstrapping the host tools, `make check-native` runs the native and
ASan/UBSan suites, installed SDK consumers, the normal native test targets,
and extracted runtime/dev/tools/tests pkgbase archives. All staging is under
/usr/obj. The focused `test-installed.py --packages` command runs the SDK and
package portion. Native-host checks use installed kernel-matched base libraries;
this does not validate an unfinished world's libc/libsys against an old kernel.

BSDTime source migration
------------------------
test-provider.py builds libtimecmp and BSDTime, then runs the real C provider
and client with simulated clock gates on native channels. It also runs policy
configuration and legacy validator ABI tests. A separate gate_test exercises
real clock gates only when explicitly enabled inside a disposable VM.
The public C ABI stays at major 1, with timecmp_client_create for held channels.
The wire format is bsd.time.v1; mixed legacy/FIDL peers fail before clock writes.
See docs/book/src/providers/time.md for versioning and coordinated rollback.
This is source migration evidence, not an installed-plane validation claim.

Use test-provider.py --sanitize for ASan/UBSan coverage of the C client,
provider/configuration parser, generated bridge, codec runtime and libchannel.
All 38 host-safe cases run with fatal sanitizer errors. The harness uses
installed service/support libraries without instrumentation and disables the
unavailable LeakSanitizer. Clock writes remain simulated. check-native includes
this run; real-gate and activation/rollback VM requirements still apply.

The ordinary provider check also builds BSDTimectl and stages libtimecmp,
libfidl and libchannel. C11/C++20 consumers compile with only installed public
headers, link shared/static libraries and exercise constructor ownership,
native-channel client creation/destruction, argument validation and the retained
legacy validator ABI. These probes do not replace a complete provider round
trip: the provider ATF suite supplies that evidence; plane activation remains
unverified.

Debug/manual/provider archive validation
----------------------------------------
`test-provider.py --packages` builds a fresh isolated debug-enabled component
stage, creates 18 pkgbase archives and checks their extracted payloads. This
covers FIDL runtime/development/tools/tests, libtimecmp, BSDTime/BSDTimectl and
provider tests, including manual aliases, complete metadata-to-archive ownership,
licenses, development/runtime dependencies, DWARF and debug-link CRCs. It runs
the two FIDL test programs, 38 time/provider cases and C11/C++20 API consumers.
The real-clock test's default opt-in guard is verified separately, not counted
as a passed real-gate test. check-native includes this package run.

`test-provider-packages.py --stage <provider-root>` runs the package portion.
Use `--verify-work <package-validation-directory>` to recheck existing archives
without rebuilding. Artifacts stay under /usr/obj. The runtime package contains
only the BSDTime/BSDTimectl subset; it is not a complete world package. Nothing
is installed, published or activated. Full world/release and VM gates remain.

BSDTime's provider tests now include the bounded kqueue session reactor:
idle/nonreading-peer isolation, copied policy labels, 32-session total and
eight-per-label admission limits, idle expiry and 512 connect/disconnect cycles
under a 256-descriptor limit. All 38 provider/policy/legacy cases pass native
and ASan/UBSan runs. This is a lifecycle regression, not a sustained soak or
proof of capability-plane activation and quiescing.

Opt-in provider resource soak
-----------------------------
`make check-soak` runs the ordinary provider tests followed by a 600-second
native soak and a 300-second ASan/UBSan soak. FIDL_SOAK_SECONDS and
FIDL_SANITIZER_SOAK_SECONDS override those durations. The focused command is
`test-provider.py [--sanitize] --soak-seconds N` (0 disables, maximum 86400).
Durations below 30 seconds are explicitly reported as smoke checks.

The installed bsdtime-tests auxiliary executable is
`/usr/tests/usr.sbin/BSDTime/resource_soak N`; it is not registered in Kyua.
It runs the real public C client, generated bindings and provider reactor over
native channels in capability mode. Eight concurrent clients (four allowed,
four denied) and four idle peers repeatedly reconnect under a 256-FD ceiling.
Every epoch checks all operation results, gate-call accounting and recovery
to the baseline FD count. A ten-second stalled epoch fails the run.

JSON lines report calls, sessions, live allocated bytes and peak RSS. After a
ten-second warmup, live allocation growth above 1 MiB fails; native peak RSS
growth above 64 MiB also fails. A fresh child excludes launcher pre-exec RSS.
ASan's quarantine affects RSS, so its RSS is reported but not gated; live
allocation is checked through the sanitizer allocator API. The child exits if
its supervising process dies. The extracted-package check runs a one-second
smoke probe and verifies the executable and split debug symbols are owned by
bsdtime-tests and bsdtime-tests-dbg.

Clock writes and syslog are simulated. This isolates resource behavior of the
client/provider path; it does not validate real clock gates, the logging sink,
capability-plane activation, kernel memory growth, comparative performance or
multi-day operation. LeakSanitizer remains unavailable on this host.

Conformance, evolution, closure, performance and VM tools (2026-10-10):
build-generators.py also builds the pinned gidl tool. generate-conformance.py
compiles contrib/fuchsia/src/tests/fidl/conformance_suite unchanged, removes
resource/protocol declarations only from the IR used for native bindings, and
emits the upstream GIDL C++ cases; lib/libfidl/tests/conformance supplies a
googletest zxtest shim and a conformance_utils.h that uses the native
transport's encode/decode entry points. build-runtime.py --test runs it
(1,033 host cases; the 193 Zircon-handle cases are compiled out), the
evolve:1/evolve:2 C peer test (lib/libfidl/tests/evolution_test.py) and
check-closure.py, which fails if a libfidl_bsd member becomes unreachable.
build-runtime.py --bench (make check-bench) records raw libchannel, C++ and C
request/reply latency, throughput and heap allocations in bench/bench.json.
vm-provider.sh boots a NO_ROOT stage with the capability plane on, checks
BSDTime through the live broker and runs Kyua (see its header). It uses the
first runnable emulator from QEMU=, the installed port, then an unpatched
local rebuild; the installed package currently needs libusb.so.4, which the
installed base (libusb.so.3) does not provide.

